  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 319 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nAn impressive look on water vapor imagery this morning, with a deep \nupper low over southern California beginning to absorb Hurricane \nPolo as it makes landfall over Baja California. Broad southwesterly \nflow is in place ahead of the low all the way eastward to the \nPlains, allowing tropical moisture to stream northwards. Yesterday \nevening's 0z sounding here at TOP had a PWAT of 1.27\" - most \nguidance takes that all the way up to around 2.0\" by tonight's 0z \nsounding. This will set the stage for multiple rounds of heavy rain \nas the upper low approaches the Plains over the next couple days. \nThe first round arrives this afternoon and evening as a weaker lead \nwave approaches the Plains. While most of the area should see some \nrain with this first round, the most widespread rain and best \npotential for local 1-3\" totals by tomorrow morning should be across \nnorth-central KS. \n\nAfter perhaps a bit of a lull Wednesday morning, the focus will \nremain across north-central KS for the next round of showers and \nstorms. Mid-level dry slot nosing in across western KS should help \nan axis of instability develop across western/central KS, though the \nextent of this will depend on coverage of earlier clouds and rain. \nAt any rate, expecting another round of convection to develop \ntowards central KS during the afternoon and evening. If around 1000 \nJ/kg of CAPE can develop just west of the area, it's not out of the \nquestion for a few stronger storms with isolated damaging winds. The \nheavy rain will remain the main hazard though. Good confidence in \nsome more pockets of 1-3\" towards north-central KS, given deep \nmoisture, weak instability, and strong synoptic forcing. \n\nWednesday night may see another brief lull in rain coverage before \nmore widespread rain redevelops early Thursday. This will be as the \nbest mid-level height falls overspread the area with the approach of \nthe upper low and a cold front begins to shift southeast across the \narea. In contrast with the first two rounds, which should see less \nof an impact across east-central KS, this last round will see heavy \nrain potential across the entire area through Thursday evening.\n\nWhile the recent trends in model guidance have actually been a \nslight shift back to the south, the total QPF forecast (Tuesday \nthrough Thursday night) remains a general 1-3\" across a majority of \nthe area. The greatest potential for local amounts to around 5\" \nstill appears to be across north-central and northeast KS where the \nFlood Watch remains in effect, as this area is most at risk for \nmultiple rounds of heavy rain.\n\nRain chances will drop off in the wake of the cold front, with just \na few leftover light showers by Friday morning. In contrast to the \nhighs in the 80s today and tomorrow, highs Friday through Sunday \nbehind the front look to stay in the 60s to low 70s. A drier airmass \nand clearing skies should help overnight lows drop into the 50s.",
